%[text] %[text:anchor:T_C8173E54] # Interpolation of Numerical Data
%[text] *Interpolation* is the process of estimating values *between* known data points. When we have a set of discrete measurements and need a value at a point we did not sample, interpolation gives us a principled estimate. This reading introduces two common interpolation methods — **linear** and **cubic spline** — and shows how to use MATLAB's `interp1` and `spline` functions to apply them.
%[text] Interpolation differs from *curve fitting*. Interpolation produces a function that passes through every data point exactly. Curve fitting produces a function that is "close to" the data points without necessarily touching any of them, and is more appropriate for noisy data. Curve fitting is covered in a separate reading.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_540F60F0] ## Interpolation Problem Statement
%[text] Given a set of known data points $(x\_1, y\_1), (x\_2, y\_2), \\ldots, (x\_n, y\_n)$, the goal of interpolation is to construct a function $\\hat{y}(x)$ that passes through these points and can be used to estimate $y$ at any intermediate value of $x$.
%[text] A useful way to think about interpolation is as "table lookup": we have a table of $x$ values and a corresponding table of $y$ values, and we want to estimate $y$ for an $x$ that does not appear in the table.
%[text] To illustrate, we will use a small set of seven measurements tabulated at integer values of $x$:
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,-1,7,-1,86]}
%[text] | **Given** |  |  | **Find** |  |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | <p align="center">$x$</p> | <p align="center">$y$</p> |  | <p align="center">$x$</p> | <p align="center">$\\hat{y}$</p> |
%[text] | 0 | 0      |  |  |  |
%[text] | 1 |  0\.8415 |  |  |  |
%[text] | 2 |  0\.9093 |  |  |  |
%[text] |  |  |  | 2\.5 | <p align="center">?</p> |
%[text] | 3 |  0\.1411 |  |  |  |
%[text] | 4 | \-0.7568 |  |  |  |
%[text] |  |  |  | 4\.1342 | <p align="center">?</p> |
%[text] | 5 | \-0.9589 |  |  |  |
%[text] | 6 | \-0.2794 |  |  |  |
%[text:table]
%[text] *(These happen to be values of* $\\sin(x)$ *rounded to four decimal places, but the interpolation methods do not know that — they only see the seven points.)*
clear; clc; close all;

% Tabulated data — seven samples
x = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
y = [0, 0.8415, 0.9093, 0.1411, -0.7568, -0.9589, -0.2794];

% Plot the data points
figure;
plot(x, y, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 8);
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
title('Sample Data Points');
grid on;
%[text] We have seven known points. The interpolation question is: given an $x$ value somewhere between 0 and 6, what is the corresponding $y$?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1F74068E] ## Linear Interpolation
%[text] **Linear interpolation** is the simplest method. Between any two adjacent data points $(x\_i, y\_i)$ and $(x\_{i+1}, y\_{i+1})$ it assumes that the unknown value lies on the straight line connecting them:
%[text] $\\hat y = y\_i + \\frac{y\_{i+1}-y\_i}{x\_{i+1}-x\_i}\\cdot(x-x\_i)$
%[text] In MATLAB, linear interpolation is the default behavior of the [`interp1`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/interp1.html) function:
%[text] `vq = interp1(x, v, xq)`
%[text] where `x` and `v` are the known data and `xq` is the query point (or vector of query points) at which we want estimates.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_436F4EE2] ### Example 1: Single Query Point
%[text] Estimate $y$ at $x = 2.5$:
xq = 2.5;
yq_lin = interp1(x, y, xq)

figure();
plot(x, y, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 8); hold on
plot(xq, yq_lin, 'bd', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'b', 'MarkerSize', 10);
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
title('Linear Interpolation at a Single Query Point');
legend('Data Points', 'Interpolated Point', 'Location', 'best');
grid on; hold off;
%[text] The returned value is the point at $x = 2.5$ on the straight line drawn between the data points at $x = 2$ and $x = 3$.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C62F5BBA] ### Example 2: Many Query Points (Vectorized)
%[text] `interp1` is *vectorized*: pass a vector of query points and it returns a vector of interpolated values. Here we build a dense set of query points spanning the full range with `linspace`:
N = 30;
xq = linspace(0, 6, N);
yq_lin = interp1(x, y, xq);

figure()
plot(x, y, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 8); hold on
plot(xq, yq_lin, 'b.', 'LineWidth', 1.5);
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
title('Linear Interpolation over Many Query Points');
legend('Data Points', 'Linear Interpolation', 'Location', 'best');
grid on; hold off;
%[text] The result is a piecewise-linear curve — straight line segments joining each pair of data points.
%[text] ### 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E3D0A94A] ## Cubic Spline Interpolation
%[text] Linear interpolation has a sharp "corner" at each data point. **Cubic spline interpolation** produces a smooth curve by fitting a separate cubic polynomial between each pair of adjacent points:
%[text] $S\_i(x) = a\_i\\cdot x^3 + b\_i\\cdot x^2 + c\_i\\cdot x + d\_i$
%[text] The coefficients are chosen so that the segments join continuously *and* so that the first and second derivatives match across each join. The result is a single smooth curve passing through every data point with no visible kinks.
%[text] In MATLAB the function is [`spline`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/spline.html):
%[text] `yq = spline(x, y, xq)`
%[text] You can also call `interp1(x, y, xq, 'spline')` — the result is the same.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8723D01B] ### Example 1: Basic Cubic Spline
%[text] Re-using the same query points from the previous example:
yq_spline = spline(x, y, xq);

figure;
plot(x, y, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 8); hold on
plot(xq, yq_spline, 'b.', 'LineWidth', 1.5, 'MarkerSize', 10)
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
title('Cubic Spline Interpolation');
legend('Data Points', 'Cubic Spline', 'Location', 'best');
grid on; hold off;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B1C532BF] ### Example 2: Denser Query Points
%[text] To see the smoothness of the spline more clearly, increase the number of query points:
xqq = linspace(0, 6, 100);
yqq = spline(x, y, xqq);

figure;
plot(x, y, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 8); hold on
plot(xqq, yqq, 'b.', 'LineWidth', 1.5)
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
title('Cubic Spline at 100 Query Points');
legend('Data Points', 'Cubic Spline', 'Location', 'best');
grid on; hold off;
%[text] With enough query points the curve appears continuous to the eye.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A863FE5F] ## Linear vs. Cubic Spline
%[text] Plotting both methods together on the same axes makes the difference clear:
xq = linspace(0, 6, 100);
yq_linear = interp1(x, y, xq);
yq_spline = spline(x, y, xq);

figure;
plot(x, y, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 8); hold on
plot(xq, yq_linear, 'g.', 'LineWidth', 1.5)
plot(xq, yq_spline, 'b.', 'LineWidth', 1.5)
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
title('Linear vs. Cubic Spline Interpolation');
legend('Data Points', 'Linear', 'Cubic Spline', 'Location', 'best');
grid on; hold off;
%[text] - **Linear** passes through every data point along straight segments — easy to compute, but the curve has corners.
%[text] - **Cubic spline** passes through every data point with a smooth curve — better when the underlying process is expected to be smooth, but can overshoot between widely-spaced points. \
%%
%[text] ## Other Methods Available in `interp1`
%[text] The full form of `interp1` allows you to specify the method as a fourth argument:
%[text] `vq = interp1(x, v, xq, method)`
%[text] Common choices include:
%[text] - `'nearest'` — returns the value of the nearest known sample (a stair-step curve).
%[text] - `'linear'` — straight line between adjacent samples (the default).
%[text] - `'pchip'` — shape-preserving piecewise cubic that does not overshoot.
%[text] - `'spline'` — smooth cubic spline (same result as the `spline` function). \
%[text] ![](text:image:7502)
%[text] We can compare all four on a single plot using a loop:
xq = linspace(0, 6, 200);
methods = {'nearest', 'linear', 'pchip', 'spline'};

figure;
plot(x, y, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 8, 'DisplayName', 'Data Points');
hold on
for k = 1:length(methods)
    yq = interp1(x, y, xq, methods{k});
    plot(xq, yq, '.', 'LineWidth', 1.5, 'DisplayName', methods{k});
end
xlabel('x');
ylabel('y');
title('Comparison of interp1 Methods');
legend('Location', 'best');
grid on; hold off;

xlim([0.00 2.72])
ylim([0.09 1.00])
legend(["Data Points", "nearest", "linear", "pchip", "spline"], "Position", [0.5715 0.2682 0.1647, 0.2404])
%[text] Each method gives a different curve through the same data points. The right choice depends on what the data represents and how smooth the underlying relationship is expected to be.
%%
%[text] ## A Note on Extrapolation
%[text] By default `interp1` returns `NaN` for query points *outside* the range of the input data:
xq_out = [-0.5, 6.5];
yq_default = interp1(x, y, xq_out)
%[text] To extrapolate, pass `'extrap'` as a fifth argument (or supply a scalar fill value):
yq_extrap = interp1(x, y, xq_out, 'linear', 'extrap')
%[text] Extrapolation should be used cautiously — the values it produces are not supported by data and can be very wrong, especially with high-order methods like splines.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1D32451A] ## Summary
%[text] This reading introduced interpolation as a way to estimate values between known data points and demonstrated two main methods in MATLAB:
%[text] - **Linear** (`interp1` with the default `'linear'`) — piecewise-linear, simple, easy to understand, but has corners at each data point.
%[text] - **Cubic spline** (`spline`, or `interp1` with `'spline'`) — smooth piecewise cubics joined with continuous first and second derivatives. \
%[text] We also saw that `interp1` supports several other methods (`'nearest'`, `'pchip'`) and that extrapolation outside the data range is possible but should be done with care.
%[text] Interpolation differs from **curve fitting**, which finds a function that approximates the data without passing through every point — useful when data is noisy. Curve fitting is covered in a separate reading.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_STEAM] ## Example: Steam Table Lookup
%[text] Linear interpolation is the typial method for **steam tables** and other tabulated thermodynamic property data. Tables are sampled at discrete pressures or temperatures, but real operating conditions land *between* the rows — exactly the situation interpolation is designed for. A short excerpt from the saturated-water pressure table looks like this:
%[text] ![](text:image:5ce4)
%[text:table]{"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | P (MPa) | T\_sat (°C) | h\_g (kJ/kg) |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | 0\.6 | 158\.83 | 2756\.2 |
%[text] | 0\.8 | 170\.41 | 2769\.1 |
%[text] | 1\.0 | 179\.88 | 2777\.1 |
%[text] | 1\.2 | 187\.96 | 2783\.8 |
%[text] | 1\.4 | 195\.04 | 2788\.9 |
%[text:table]
%[text] Suppose a steam plant is operating at $P = 0.85$ MPa and we need the saturation temperature $T\_{sat}$ and the saturated-vapor enthalpy $h\_g$ at that pressure. Neither value appears directly in the table, so we interpolate between the rows for 0.8 MPa and 1.0 MPa:
%[text] 
% Saturated water — excerpt from a pressure-based steam table
P_tab    = [0.6,    0.8,    1.0,    1.2,    1.4   ]; % MPa
Tsat_tab = [158.83, 170.41, 179.88, 187.96, 195.04]; % deg C
hg_tab   = [2756.2, 2769.1, 2777.1, 2783.8, 2788.9]; % kJ/kg

% Operating pressure
P_op = 0.85; % MPa

% Linear interpolation for each property
Tsat = interp1(P_tab, Tsat_tab, P_op)
hg   = interp1(P_tab, hg_tab,   P_op)

% Plot both lookups
figure;
subplot(1, 2, 1)
plot(P_tab, Tsat_tab, 'ko-', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'k'); hold on
plot(P_op, Tsat, 'rd', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 10);
xlabel('P (MPa)'); ylabel('T_{sat} (\circC)');
title('Saturation Temperature');
legend('Tabulated', 'Interpolated', 'Location', 'northwest');
grid on; hold off;

subplot(1, 2, 2)
plot(P_tab, hg_tab, 'ko-', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'k'); hold on
plot(P_op, hg, 'rd', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r', 'MarkerSize', 10);
xlabel('P (MPa)'); ylabel('h_g (kJ/kg)');
title('Saturated-Vapor Enthalpy');
legend('Tabulated', 'Interpolated', 'Location', 'southeast');
grid on; hold off;
%[text] Linear interpolation is the convention for steam-table lookups because the tables are sampled finely enough that the property variation is nearly linear over a single interval — a cubic spline would be more work without meaningfully changing the answer. This is how every thermodynamics textbook problem that says "at $P = 0.85$ MPa, find..." is actually solved.

